1
00:00:00,340 --> 00:00:05,362
An engineering manager has three coding
tools available and a team whose small

2
00:00:05,362 --> 00:00:07,294
fixes sit in the review queue.

3
00:00:07,474 --> 00:00:12,514
For this fictional order service, the
business task is to make routine validator

4
00:00:12,514 --> 00:00:15,539
fixes easier to review while preserving
quality.

5
00:00:15,719 --> 00:00:20,309
The manager starts with a real kind of
change: a ticket that spells out which

6
00:00:20,309 --> 00:00:21,680
quantities should pass.

7
00:00:21,860 --> 00:00:24,840
This gives the team a clear way to judge
the result.

8
00:00:26,298 --> 00:00:28,583
The ticket gives us four examples.

9
00:00:28,763 --> 00:00:30,366
One and five are valid.

10
00:00:30,546 --> 00:00:33,259
Zero and negative one should be rejected.

11
00:00:33,439 --> 00:00:37,556
We also want a small diff and a test that
fails before the fix.

12
00:00:37,736 --> 00:00:43,268
For a manager, these are useful acceptance
criteria because a reviewer can check them

13
00:00:43,268 --> 00:00:44,179
independently.

14
00:00:44,359 --> 00:00:48,326
Look for equally clear examples in the
tasks you want to pilot.

15
00:00:48,506 --> 00:00:51,908
The team can discuss quality using the
same evidence.

16
00:00:53,382 --> 00:00:58,447
Copilot in Visual Studio Code is already
where the author edits and checks diffs.

17
00:00:58,627 --> 00:01:03,648
Claude Code and OpenCode are available in
the approved corporate Google Cloud

18
00:01:03,648 --> 00:01:04,300
workspace.

19
00:01:04,480 --> 00:01:10,014
That remote workspace is a sensible option
for tasks that genuinely need its tooling.

20
00:01:10,194 --> 00:01:13,770
Here, the small change fits the existing
editor workflow.

21
00:01:13,950 --> 00:01:18,592
We choose Copilot for the pilot and
reserve the remote tools for a separate

22
00:01:18,592 --> 00:01:20,510
test with a reason to use them.

23
00:01:21,965 --> 00:01:24,679
The model is a separate choice from the
editor.

24
00:01:24,859 --> 00:01:28,412
Suppose two model options are enabled by
policy.

25
00:01:28,592 --> 00:01:33,673
Give them the same eight synthetic tasks,
the same instructions, and the same time

26
00:01:33,673 --> 00:01:34,107
budget.

27
00:01:34,287 --> 00:01:39,429
Ask a reviewer to score each final diff
against the written acceptance criteria.

28
00:01:39,609 --> 00:01:43,118
Capture how long the author had to steer
or repair the work.

29
00:01:43,298 --> 00:01:47,469
Model A and model B are placeholders for
your approved choices.

30
00:01:48,923 --> 00:01:54,010
In this illustration, model A met all
criteria on seven of eight tasks.

31
00:01:54,190 --> 00:01:56,028
Model B met them on eight.

32
00:01:56,208 --> 00:01:58,847
The author spent a little longer with
model B.

33
00:01:59,027 --> 00:02:04,099
Since the team agreed that every boundary
check must pass for this small benchmark,

34
00:02:04,099 --> 00:02:05,810
model B is the pilot choice.

35
00:02:05,990 --> 00:02:08,852
Keep the scores and the sample size
together.

36
00:02:09,032 --> 00:02:13,699
Eight synthetic tasks give a practical
starting point, and the pilot will test

37
00:02:13,699 --> 00:02:15,195
how that choice holds up.

38
00:02:16,673 --> 00:02:20,311
Write down the decision while it is still
easy to reconstruct.

39
00:02:20,491 --> 00:02:25,532
We are using Copilot in the editor with
the second approved model for tightly

40
00:02:25,532 --> 00:02:27,038
scoped validator fixes.

41
00:02:27,218 --> 00:02:29,577
The engineering lead owns the pilot.

42
00:02:29,757 --> 00:02:32,267
A human reviews every proposed change.

43
00:02:32,447 --> 00:02:37,993
Revisit the choice if the approved model
changes, scope expands, or review quality

44
00:02:37,993 --> 00:02:38,399
slips.

45
00:02:38,579 --> 00:02:44,041
The next episode turns those expectations
into explicit boundaries for an agent.
